  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced Displacement and Mobility Tracking and Multi-Sectoral Support for Displaced Populations in Nigeria.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The result of the conflict in the North-East and North-West zones of Nigeria remains a large-scale humanitarian and protection crisis and one of the most severe in the world, characterized by forced displacement and human rights violations. Over the past thirteen years, the crisis has impacted the lives, livelihoods, and well-being of millions of people. In addition to the armed conflict in the North-East and farmer-herder conflicts/criminal activities in the North-West, climate change has increased vulnerability in Nigeria, particularly for internally displaced persons (IDPs) and host communities. The continuous influx of IDPs from previously occupied areas or camp closures further strains living conditions, causing inadequate services and facilities. Given this context, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting IDPs, returnees and host communities, as well as humanitarian partners, through a multisectoral intervention that will focus on the provision of lifesaving direct assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
